# An ARM Cortex-M3 that needs no hardware: the board QEMU emulates as
# `lm3s6965evb` (a Stellaris LM3S6965, 256 KB of flash, 64 KB of RAM).
#
#     zeno build --board boards/qemu-cortex-m3 examples/blink.zeno
#
# Zeno's own tests build programs for it, to see that the C `zeno build`
# writes does on a 32-bit chip what it does on a computer. It is also
# the pattern for the folder of a real board: docs/board-build.md.

about "An ARM Cortex-M3 that QEMU emulates (its machine lm3s6965evb)"

# The C compiler for ARM chips with no system on them, and the
# processor it makes code for.
compiler "arm-none-eabi-gcc"
flags "-mcpu=cortex-m3" "-mthumb"

# Each function in a part of its own, so that the linker can leave out
# the ones the program never calls.
flags "-ffunction-sections" "-fdata-sections"

# This board has no header of its own: its pins are the simulated ones,
# as on a computer (docs/board.md). The simulated board sleeps and reads
# the clock the POSIX way, and system.c has the two functions for it.
flags "-D_POSIX_TIMERS=1" "-D_POSIX_MONOTONIC_CLOCK=1"

# What runs before `main`, and what the C library asks of a system.
sources "startup.c" "system.c"

# Where the flash and the RAM of the chip are.
script "link.ld"

# The start is startup.c, not the one that comes with the compiler.
link "-nostartfiles" "-Wl,--gc-sections"

# QEMU runs the program. What it prints comes out of the first serial
# port, which `-nographic` joins to the terminal.
run "qemu-system-arm" "-M" "lm3s6965evb" "-nographic" "-semihosting" "-kernel"
